course: dictionary
lesson: 3091
title: Softer words, strong feelings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words are rude, and people do not say them at school. Writers sometimes avoid the rude word by using a softer one that sounds similar. «"Eff off" is a rude British way to tell someone to go away, softened from the f-word.» The rude word is not written out, but the meaning is clear: the speaker is cross and wants someone gone.
explain: Context helps you spot the feeling. The words around «eff» tell you whether it is being used rudely. «Words like "freaking", "flipping" and "effing" work the same way for the strongest swear word, the f-word, so the speaker sounds cross but not filthy.» So when you see a softer word like this, ask: is the character angry? The answer is usually yes.
explain: Eff has another, harmless meaning. It is also the name of the letter F. When you spell or read aloud a letter, eff is simply that letter. The context shows which meaning is right: a rude sentence means the swear-word stand-in, and a spelling lesson means the letter.
explain: Even softened swear words are not for school, so you should never copy them into your work or say them out loud. If you meet one in a book, you can understand the feeling without repeating the word. Read the clues, know what the character feels, and then move on.
fact: Eff is a euphemism, which means a softer word used in place of a rude one.
fact: «Eff off» means go away, said angrily, and it is rude.
fact: «Effing» is a less rude form, used to sound cross, like «freaking» and «flipping».
fact: Eff is also the name of the letter F.
fact: Rude words are not for school, even when they are softened.
exercise: «Eff off» is a rude British way to tell someone to go away. What does «eff off» mean? | Come and play with me | Say sorry in a kind voice | Stop and wait for the bus | *Go away, said angrily
exercise: In spelling, «eff» is the name of which letter? | K | *F | G | E
exercise: Words like «freaking» and «flipping» are used instead of a strong swear word. Why do writers use them? | To say a word in another language | *To sound cross without being filthy | To make the text harder to read | To show the speaker is very happy
test: Which word is a polite-sounding stand-in for a very rude swear word? | Garden | *Eff | Table | Window
test: When «eff» names a letter, what is it? | A kind of cake | A way to say go away | *The letter F | A swear word
exam: Why should you never say rude swear words at school, even softened ones? | Because they are the names of letters | Because teachers find them funny | *Because they are rude and not for school | Because they are long words
